                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#logical_definition"} Logical definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#other_characterizations"} Other characterizations
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#InTermsOfSheafTopoi"} In terms of sheaf topoi
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#FunctorialDefinition"} Functorial definition
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#hybrid_definition"} Hybrid definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#localization"} Localization
              %li
                %a{:href => "#morphisms_of_theories"} Morphisms of theories
              %li
                %a{:href => "#gros_categories_of_models"} Gros categories of models
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em geometric theory
            has many different incarnations. A few are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A geometric theory is a (possibly infinitary)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first order
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} models
                are preserved and reflected by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            %li
              %p
                A geometric theory is a (possibly infinitary)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first order
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                whose axioms can be written as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequents
                in context of formulae constructed from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connective"} connectives
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"}> truth
                ),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                (finite
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} conjunction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsity"}> falsity
                ),
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
                (possibly infinitary
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"}> existential quantification
                ), as well as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"}> equality
                ).
            %li
              %p
                A geometric theory is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
                description of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %p
            The equivalence of these statements involves some serious proofs, including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            characterizing Grothendieck topoi.
          %h2#logical_definition Logical definition
          %p
            In logical terms, a geometric theory fits into a hierarchy of theories that can be interpreted in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a hierarchy of types of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            in the internal logic are represented by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
                and identities (top elements), and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =
                is represented by equalizer, these can be interpreted in any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+category"} lex category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+theory"} cartesian theories
            %li
              %p
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                is represented by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                of a subobject, it can be interpreted in any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∧
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                are
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theories
            %li
              %p
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+elements"} bottom elements
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theories
            %li
              %p
                Finally, theories which also involve infinitary
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+coherent+categories"} infinitary coherent categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+categories"} geometric categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong geometric theories
          %p Note that the axioms of one of these theories are actually of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi \vdash_{\vec{x}} \psi
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            are formulas involving only the specified connectives and quantifiers,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            means entailment, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{x}
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \vec{x}. (\varphi \Rightarrow \psi)
          %p
            so that although
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            are not strictly part of any of the above logics, they can be applied “once at top level.” In an axiom of this form for geometric logic, the formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            which must be built out of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            are sometimes called
            %em positive
            or
            %em geometric
            formulas.
          %p
            The interpretation of arbitrary uses of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            requires a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} well-powered
            is automatically a Heyting category, but
            %em geometric functors
            are not necessarily Heyting functors. Likewise, a well-powered geometric category automatically has arbitrary intersections of subobjects as well, so we can interpret infinitary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigwedge
            in its internal logic, but again these are not preserved by geometric functors.
          %p
            By the usual syntactic constructions (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            generates a “free geometric category containing a model of that theory,” also known as its
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_T
            has the universal property that for any other geometric category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_T \to G'
            are equivalent to
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G'
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                be a signature, then the set of all geometric sequents over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                as well as the empty set are geometric theories called the
                %em inconsistent theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝕋
                        %mn 0
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^\Sigma_0
                resp. the
                %em empty theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝕋
                        %mn 1
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^\Sigma_1
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo ⊤
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\top\vdash\bot\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝕋
                        %mn 0
                        %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^\emptyset_0
                is classified by the initial topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝕋
                        %mn 1
                        %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}^\emptyset _1
                is classified by the terminal topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                (see at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
                for details).
            %li
              %p
                The empty signature occurring in the preceding example is an extreme case of a
                %em propositional signature
                i.e. one lacking sort symbols. These can at most contain 0-ary relation symbols since function symbols and higher order relation symbols require sorts. Whence theories over such signatures can at most contain sequents in the empty context between formulas consisting of quantifier-free nestings of 0-ary relation symbols and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge,\bigvee
                and are therefor called
                %em propositional theories
                \- in other words: geometric logic boils down to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+topos"} localic toposes
                (see there for further information).
            %li
              %p
                Any finitary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                is, in particular, a cartesian theory, and hence geometric. This includes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+categories"}
                  theory
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕂
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
                  of categories
                with models the (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"}> strict
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                is not finitary-algebraic, but it is cartesian, and hence geometric; this generalises to (finitary)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theories
            %li
              %p
                The theory of
                = succeed "-free" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion"} torsion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n\cdot x = 0) \vdash_{x} (x = 0)
                for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\in \mathbb{N}
            %li
              %p
                The theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+rings"} local rings
                is a coherent theory, being obtained from the theory of commutative rings by adding the sequent
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0 = 1) \vdash \bot
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists z. ((x+y)z = 1) \vdash_{x y} (\exists z.(x z = 1) \vee \exists z.(y z = 1))
              %p
                saying that if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+y
                is invertible, then either
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                is so.
            %li
              %p
                The theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fields"} fields
                is also coherent, being obtained from the theory of commutative rings by adding
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0 = 1) \vdash \bot
                and also
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi y
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash_{x} (x=0) \vee (\exists y.(x y = 1))
              %p
                asserting that every element is either zero or invertible. In the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive logic
                that holds internal to the categories in question, this is the notion of a “discrete field;” other classically equivalent axiomatizations (called “Heyting fields” or “residue fields” – see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %li
              %p
                The theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion"} torsion
                abelian groups is geometric but not coherent; it can be obtained from the theory of abelian groups by adding the sequent
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi x
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash_{x} \bigvee_{n\ge 1} (n \cdot x = 0)
              %p
                asserting that for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=0
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+x=0
                or
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+x+x=0
                or …. Similarly, the theory of fields of finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
                is geometric but not coherent.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em theory of the standard successor algebra
                is geometric. Its signature has one sort symbol
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s:N\to N
                and one constant
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo :
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0:N
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number+object"} natural number objects
                by taking advantage of the availability of infinitary disjunctions in geometric logic in order to assert in the third axiom that every natural number is
                %em standard
                i.e. either 0 or obtained from 0 by (repeated) application of the successor function:
              %ul
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi n
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = s(n) \vdash_{n} \bot
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mi n
                            %mo ′
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mi n
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n) = s(n') \vdash_{n n'} n=n'
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi n
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                          %mi i
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %munder
                          %munder
                            %mrow
                              %mi s
                              %mi …
                              %mi s
                            %mo ⏟
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash_{n} \bigvee_{i} n=\underset{i}{\underbrace{s \dots s}}(0)
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                %em geometric
                theory of natural numbers is
                = succeed "!" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+theory"} complete
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                is the terminal topos, classifying also the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+theory"} empty theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                of natural numbers “comes for free” in geometric logic. This observation underlies the ideas for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+type+theory"} geometric type theory
                (for more on this see there or the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Vickers09"} article by Vickers
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
                is geometric. This is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional
                theory, having no sorts, and having one relation symbol “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\lt x\lt q
                ” for each pair of rational numbers
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\lt q
              %ul
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi max
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi min
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p_1\lt x\lt q_1) \wedge (p_2\lt x\lt q_2) \vdash (\max(p_1,p_2) \lt x \lt \min(q_1,q_2))
                  (if
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi max
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi min
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi q
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi q
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \max(p_1,p_2)\lt \min(q_1,q_2)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 2
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %msub
                          %mi q
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p_1\lt x\lt q_1) \wedge (p_2\lt x\lt q_2) \vdash \bot
                  (otherwise).
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ′
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p\lt x\lt q) \vdash (p\lt x\lt q') \vee (p' \lt x\lt q)
                  (if
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi p
                          %mo ′
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi q
                          %mo ′
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi q
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\lt p' \lt q' \lt q
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mi p
                            %mo ′
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mi q
                            %mo ′
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ′
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p\lt x\lt q) \vdash \bigvee_{p\lt p'\lt q'\lt q} (p' \lt x\lt q')
                  \.
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                        %mo ⊢
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                            %mo &lt;
                            %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash \bigvee_{p\lt q} (p\lt x\lt q)
                  \.
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
                of this theory is the topos of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
                on the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %li
              %p
                The theory of flat diagrams
                %sup#fnref:1
                  %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
                over a small category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                is geometric. For each object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                there is a sort
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_i
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon i \to j
                a function symbol
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi u
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi i
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_u\colon \sigma_i \to \sigma_j
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊤
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %msub
                                %mi σ
                                %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi α
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi Id
                                %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash_{x\colon\sigma_i} \alpha_{Id_i}(x) = x
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊤
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %msub
                                %mi σ
                                %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi α
                            %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi α
                            %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi α
                            %mrow
                              %mi v
                              %mi u
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash_{x\colon\sigma_i} \alpha_{v}(\alpha_u(x)) = \alpha_{v u}(x)
                    (
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi u
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
