                is called a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+presheaf+type"} theory of presheaf type
          %h2#other_characterizations Other characterizations
          %h3#InTermsOfSheafTopoi In terms of sheaf topoi
          %p
            Categories of each “logical” type can also be “completed” with respect to a suitable “exactness” property, without changing their internal logic. Any regular category has an completion into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+and+exact+completion"} regular and exact completion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+pretopos"} infinitary pretopos
          %p
            However,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            says that an infinitary pretopos having a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+set"} generating set
            is precisely a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and small
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            part of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* \dashv f_*
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            (the “inverse image”) preserves finite limits.
          %p Thus:
          .standout
            %p Grothendieck topoi and inverse-image functors are in some sense the “natural home” for models of geometric theories.
          %p
            Note, though, that geometric morphisms are generally considered as pointing in the direction of the
            %em direct
            image
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            are the toposes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+space"} sober topological spaces
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            respectively, then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            correspond precisely to geometric morphisms
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            (at least, as long as one works in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            for which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is an elementary topos), and hence its internal logic also includes “higher-order” constructions such as function-objects and power-objects. However, these are not preserved by geometric functors, so they (like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+functors"} logical functors
          %p
            In particular, we can apply the “exact completion” operation to the syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_T
            of a geometric theory to obtain an infinitary pretopos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[T]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            was itself small,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[T]
            will have a generating set, and therefore will be a Grothendieck topos. The universal property of the syntactic category, combined with that of the exact completion, implies that for any Grothendieck topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to Set[T]
            are equivalent to
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[T]
            is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            In the other direction, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is any small
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functors"} flat functors
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\to Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
            classifying geometric morphisms into sheaf topoi, it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            for this theory. Therefore, not only does every geometric theory have a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of
            %em some
            theory. Very different-looking theories can have equivalent classifying topoi; this of course implies that they have all the same categories of models in all Grothendieck topoi up to equivalence (hence a Grothendieck topos is the “extensional categorical essence” of a geometric theory). We say that two geometric theories with equivalent classifying topoi are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
          %h3#FunctorialDefinition Functorial definition
          %p
            We can approach the same idea by starting instead from the notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
          %p
            Suppose we consider what sorts of “theories” we can define in terms of Grothendieck topoi, that are preserved by inverse image functors. Any such theory should certainly define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi GrTop
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\colon GrTop^{op}\to Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GrTop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrTop
            is the 2-category of Grothendieck topoi and geometric morphisms, so for the moment let’s call any such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            a “theory”. The image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(E)
            of a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is supposed to be “the category of
            = succeed "-models,\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %em classifying topos
            for such a 2-functor will be just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representing+object"} representing object
            for it.
          %p
            Of course, this notion of theory is far too general; we only want to consider theories that are constructed in some reasonable way. One theory that should certainly be geometric is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+objects"} theory of objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            to itself, considered as a mere category, and an inverse image functor to itself, considered as a mere functor. The theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            can be shown to have a classifying topos, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+for+the+theory+of+objects"} object classifier
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set[O]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_n
            of
            = succeed "-tuples" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            How can we construct more theories that ought to be geometric? We should start from some finite collection of objects (i.e. a model of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to O
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %em geometric construct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a natural way.” More precisely, to every
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            it assigns an object of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Now define a
            %em simple functional extension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inserter"} inserter
            of a pair of geometric constructs
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\;\rightrightarrows\; O
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-model." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Finally, define a
            %em simple geometric quotient
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverter"} inverter
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modification"} modification
            between a pair of geometric constructs
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\;\rightrightarrows\; O
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifier"} equifiers
          %p
            From this point of view, a
            %strong geometric theory
            is a theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi GrTop
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrTop^{op}\to Cat
            obtained from some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_n
            by a finite sequence of simple functional extensions and simple geometric quotients. Of course, once we know that each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_n
            has a classifying topos, it follows immediately that any geometric theory has a classifying topos, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GrTop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrTop
            has inserters and inverters.
          %h3#hybrid_definition Hybrid definition
          %p
            The following definition is sort of a “halfway house” between logic and geometry. Start with a first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (this is the logical part). Then we have a 2-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mi Str
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi GrTop
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma Str\colon GrTop^{op}\to Cat
            sending a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mi Str
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma Str(E)
            of
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %strong geometric theory
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is defined to consist of the following.
          %ul
            %li
              For each Grothendieck topos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/replete+subcategory"} replete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(E)
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Str
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma Str(E)
            %li
              For each geometric morphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon E\to F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\in T(F)
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*M\in T(E)
              (i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a subfunctor of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Str
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma Str
            %li
              For any set-indexed jointly surjective family
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_i \colon E_i \to E)_{i\in I}
              of geometric morphisms, and any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi Str
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \in \Sigma Str(E)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i^* M \in T(E_i)
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\in T(E)
          %p The equivalence of this definition with the others can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              Olivia Caramello, “A characterization theorem for geometric logic”,
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1404"} arXiv:0912.1404
          %p
            It is not sufficient, in the third condition, to restrict to the case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a singleton, but it is sufficient to consider the case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is a singleton together with all families of coproduct injections
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_i \to \coprod_i E_i)_{i\in I}
          %h2#localization Localization
          %p
            By framing this notion in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            we can talk of geometric theories over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+topos"} bounded
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+point+of+view"} relative version
            of “Grothendieck topos”). As a simple example, if we have a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we can describe left
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            as models of a geometric theory over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-toposes." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
          %p
            Similarly to the
            = succeed "-based" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            over a topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T] \to S
            that classifies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is naturally equivalent to the category of morphisms of
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to S[T]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi Top
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Mod(E) \simeq Top_S(E,S[T])
          %h2#morphisms_of_theories Morphisms of theories
          %p
            Since the classifying topos encodes the “extensional essence” of a geometric theory, it makes sense to define a
            %strong map of theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to T'
            to be a morphism of
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: S[T'] \to S[T]
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T']
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            induces a functor,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forget
              along
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %h2#gros_categories_of_models Gros categories of models
          %p
            Define the
            %span.newWikiWord
              gros category
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/gros+category"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Mod
            of
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,A)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is an
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g): (E,A) \to (F,B)
            is given by a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: F \to E
            of
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: f^*(A) \to B
            of
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: T \to T'
            of geometric theories over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            induces a forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T' Mod \to T Mod
            which leaves unchanged the
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Mod \to T' Mod
            which may change the topos. For if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a: E \to S[T]
            is a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} pulling
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            back along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            yields a
            = succeed "-model," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            but in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limits"} 2-limits
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+topos"} bounded
            = succeed "-toposes." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            The 2-categorical version of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Mod
            is useful in generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            construction: See at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cole%27s+theory+of+spectrum"} Cole's theory of spectrum
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
              says, that if a statement in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
              is deducible from a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
              using classical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              and the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+type+theory"} geometric type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+type+theory"} geometric homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cole%27s+theory+of+spectrum"} Cole's theory of spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+for+the+theory+of+objects"} classifying topos for the theory of objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+objects"} theory of objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+decidable+objects"} theory of decidable objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+model+homomorphisms"} theory of model homomorphisms
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+flat+functors"} theory of flat diagrams
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+categories"} theory of categories
          %h2#references References
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Vickers"}> Steven Vickers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Continuity and geometric logic
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jal.2013.07.004"} doi:10.1016/j.jal.2013.07.004
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                , sections B4.2, D1.1 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"}> Michael Makkai
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+E.+Reyes"} Gonzalo E. Reyes
                %em First Order Categorical Logic
                , LNM 611, Springer Berlin 1977. (
                %a{:href => "https://marieetgonzalo.files.wordpress.com/2018/04/makkai-reyes-book.pdf"}> draft
                )
          %p A textbook account of (finitary) geometric logic can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
          %p A systematic introduction to topos theory and geometric logic can be found in the following draft by O. Caramello:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivia+Caramello"}> Olivia Caramello
              ,
              %em Topos-theoretic background
              , ms. 2014. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.oliviacaramello.com/Unification/ToposTheoreticPreliminariesOliviaCaramello.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p which has become a chapter of the following monograph exploring first-order model theory in the context of Grothendieck toposes:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivia+Caramello"}> Olivia Caramello
              ,
              %em Theories, Sites, Toposes
              , Oxford UP 2018.
          %p For additional background on (finitary) geometric formulas consider:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Karel Stokkermans,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"} Steve Vickers
                %em Catlist discussion March 1999
                \. (
                %a{:href => "http://comments.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/1058"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                H.J. Keisler:
                %em Theory of models with generalized atomic formulas
                , JSL 25 (1960), pp.1-26.
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"}> Steve Vickers
              ,
              %em Geometric logic in computer science
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~sjv/GLiCS.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion with an eye towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+type+theory"} geometric type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Vickers09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Vickers"}> Steve Vickers
              ,
              %em Locales and toposes as spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~sjv/LocTopSpaces.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion with an eye towards
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %ul
            %li
              Guillaume Raynaud,
              %em Fibred Contextual Quantum Physics
              (
              %a{:href => "https://etheses.bham.ac.uk/id/eprint/1685/"}> etheses:1685
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://etheses.bham.ac.uk//id/eprint/1685/1/Raynaud14PhD.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            for geometric theories is discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henrik+Forssell"}> Henrik Forssell
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological representation of geometric theories
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1109.0699"} arxiv/1109.0699
          %p A nice short exposition together with an unorthodox proposal to expand geometric logic with fix point operators can be found here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Blass"}> Andreas Blass
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topoi and Computation
              %strong 36
              (1988) pp.57-65. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.lsa.umich.edu/~ablass/eatcs.pdf"}> draft
              )
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  This is also sometimes called the theory of flat functors on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
